      {
        "id": "MSjSsoUKPp68VgLl",
        "title": "Biomimicry: Inspiration from Nature",
        "content": {
          "type": "Text",
          "format": "html",
          "text": [
            "<p>When you think of a robot, you probably think about something hard and rigid, like this:</p>"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "6Rvszk1aC6sLO1WU",
        "title": "Sketch 2",
        "content": {
          "type": "Drawing",
          "objects": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "nE50LatFdsssbBR_",
              "x": 533,
              "y": 58,
              "hFlip": false,
              "vFlip": false,
              "rotation": 0,
              "visible": true,
              "width": 302,
              "height": 54,
              "stroke": "#000000",
              "text": "STAND IN FOR HARD ROBOTS"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "u1CKMXu8UcHu5WNT",
        "title": "Text 2",
        "content": {
          "type": "Text",
          "format": "html",
          "text": [
            "<p>Add the stuff from: https://www.gmwgroup.harvard.edu/soft-robotics (biomimicry)</p><p>Starfish vs. robot (similarities in form and function, but also dissimilarities in exact workings)</p><p>What are the parallels vs. not parallels/differences between their prosthesis and a hand?</p><p></p><p>In the &quot;evaluate&quot; section --&gt; review these parallels/differences and think about next steps [foreshadowing EMG]</p><p></p><p>These robotics are useful in manufacturing, research, healthcare, and lots of other areas.</p><p>However, they are not as good at working with really delicate objects without crushing them (e.g., a raw egg, a live mouse).</p><p>The Whitesides Research Group at Harvard University was trying to fix that issue. They noticed that some animals in nature have ways of grasping objects that are really different, and might be better for these tasks.</p><p>They were inspired by starfish and elephant trunks to build &quot;Soft Robots.&quot; They developed special elastomeric materials (materials that are rubbery and flexible) that can bend when they&#x27;re filled with high pressure air, like a balloon. That helps them curve gently around an object without breaking it.</p><p></p><p>This robot was designed by the Whitesides Research Group at Harvard University. It&#x27;s a soft robot: it works really differently from most robots that you see that are made of metal or hard plastics.  A 9-cm tip-to-tip pneu-net gripping an uncooked chicken egg. A string suspends the gripper and assists in lifting the egg; a tube, visible on the left side of the gripper, runs into the central portion of the gripper to provide pressurized air for actuation.</p>"
          ]
        }
      },
